      <floats-group id="article-28657.s20">
        <fig id="article-28657.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Etiology of Roth Spots. The&#x000a0;table classifies the causes of Roth spots into infectious, hematologic, vasculitic, embolic, and other disorders. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Etiology__of__Roth__Spots" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28657.s21">
        <fig id="article-28657.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pathophysiology of Roth Spots.&#x000a0;The process begins with vascular injury and microvascular damage from a systemic condition, which leads to endothelial damage and breakdown of the blood-retinal barrier. This&#x000a0;event results in a hemorrhage, where blood extravasates into the superficial retina. A fibrin plug then forms and collects at the center of the bleed, creating the characteristic pale spot. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Pathophysiology__of__Roth__Spots" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28657.s22">
        <fig id="article-28657.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Microscopic Components of a Roth Spot. A Roth spot originates from a ruptured arteriole within the retinal layer, often presenting with damaged endothelium. The visible hemorrhage is contained by a layer of fibrin and filled with inflammatory cells. The characteristic pale center is the fibrin plug, surrounded by blood and associated with retinal edema. Macrophages and neutrophils migrate to the site of the hemorrhage. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Screenshot__2025-10-03__at__8.26.01xAM" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28657.s23">
        <fig id="article-28657.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Systemic Pathways Leading to Roth Spots.&#x000a0;The diagram illustrates&#x000a0;3 main systemic categories that trigger Roth spots. The infective pathway involves bacteremia, the hematologic pathway encompasses various blood abnormalities, and the metabolic pathway is represented by diabetes mellitus. Regardless of the originating pathway, the result is the development of retinal microhemorrhages&#x000a0;or Roth spots. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Systemic__Pathways__for__Roth__Spots" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28657.s24">
        <fig id="article-28657.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Management Pathway for Roth Spots.&#x000a0;The clinical pathway for Roth spots begins with a fundus examination to identify the characteristic lesions. This step is followed by a comprehensive systemic evaluation to determine the underlying cause. Etiology-based management targets the primary disease, such as infective endocarditis, hematologic disease, diabetes mellitus, or hypertension. The process concludes with follow-up retinal imaging to confirm resolution or trigger a referral for further intervention. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Screenshot__2025-10-04__at__7.39.12xAM" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-28657.s25">
        <fig id="article-28657.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Treatment Planning for Roth Spots.&#x000a0;Management begins with a fundoscopic exam and requires both a systemic diagnostic workup and further ocular evaluation. Once the underlying cause is determined (eg, infective endocarditis, hematologic disorder, diabetes, or hypertension), an etiology-specific treatment is initiated. The entire process necessitates regular monitoring of the patient's condition for therapeutic efficacy and resolution. Bharat Gurnani, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Treatment__planning" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
